      "translation_chunk_count": 0,
      "translated_text": "Survey Method A public library wanted to understand why visitors described one reading room as quiet even when the sound meter showed levels similar to those in another room. Staff designed a short acoustic survey using a calibrated handheld meter and a written observation sheet. Measurements were taken at five marked positions during three periods of the day. The meter was mounted 1.2 m above the floor and kept at least 1 m from walls or large furniture. Each sample lasted sixty seconds and recorded both the average sound level and the highest short-duration peak. The morning survey produced averages between 36.8 and 39.5 dBA During the afternoon, the values rose by roughly 4 dB as more people used the study tables. The highest peak, 61 2 dBA, occurred when a metal water bottle fell near the entrance. Surprisingly. the room that visitors considered calmer did not always have the lowest average level Observers noted that its sounds were more continuous: ventilation noise. turning pages and distant footsteps. In the other room, sudden chair movements and brief conversations were more noticeable even when the overall average remained modest Staff used the observations to test small changes rather than imposing strict silence rules Felt pads were added to twenty-four chair legs, a return trolley was moved away from the main reading area, and a soft- close strip was fitted to one cabinet door One week later the same measurement route was repeated. Average levels changed only slightly. but the number of peaks above 55 dBA fell from seventeen to nine during the busiest hour. Visitor comments also mentioned fewer abrupt interruptions. The survey showed that a single average number could not describe the entire experience of a space. Frequency, timing and character of sounds also mattered The library therefore kept the simple route as a quarterly check and added a column for notable sound events. This gave staff a repeatable way to evaluate practical changes without treating the sound meter as a complete measure of comfort The same marked positions were retained on a floor plan so that future measurements could be taken at comparable locations rather than wherever space happened to be available",
